Here is the executive-level business case—the "too good to pass up" benefits—that justify the pain of the transition over your 3-year roadmap.
________________________________________
1. The Hard ROI: Stop Paying the "Tax"
This is usually the strongest argument for the C-suite. If your organization is licensed for Microsoft 365 E3 or E5 (which is standard for enterprise M365 deployments), you are already paying for Microsoft Intune. By keeping Workspace ONE, you are paying a massive redundancy tax. Transitioning allows you to completely eliminate the Broadcom/Omnissa renewal contract. In an enterprise of your size, this consolidation often saves hundreds of thousands—if not millions—of dollars over a three-year term. That is budget you can reclaim and redirect toward your AI and Copilot rollouts.
2. Native Security vs. Bolted-On Security
Cybersecurity in 2026 relies on "Zero Trust"—verifying every single login attempt based on identity and device health.
•	The Workspace ONE way: Workspace ONE has to "talk" to Microsoft Entra ID via APIs to report if a device is safe. This creates a bridge. Bridges can break, syncs can delay, and workarounds are often required.
•	The Intune way: Intune, Entra ID, and Microsoft Defender share the exact same brain (the Microsoft Graph). If Defender detects malware on a laptop, Intune instantly flips the device to "non-compliant," and Entra ID instantly revokes the user's access to their M365 email and files. There is no latency. It is native, impenetrable security.
3. Feeding the AI Engine (Future-Proofing for 2029)
Your 3-year roadmap is going to be heavily dominated by AI. Microsoft is building Security Copilot and Copilot for IT natively into its admin centers. Because Workspace ONE is a third-party data silo, Microsoft’s AI cannot natively read its telemetry. By moving your devices to Intune, your IT operations and security teams will be able to use natural language to query your entire global fleet (e.g., "Which devices in Europe are failing the latest Windows update?"). Intune sets the data foundation required to actually use Microsoft's AI tools for IT automation.
4. The "Single Pane of Glass" for IT Operations
Right now, your IT team is managing Office 2024 installations, M365 licenses, Exchange mailboxes, and identity in Microsoft portals, but jumping into Workspace ONE to manage the physical devices. This split-brain administration requires double the training and doubles the vendor troubleshooting when something goes wrong. Standardizing on Intune unifies your entire IT administration into a single vendor ecosystem.
________________________________________
The PM Summary for Leadership: "Migrating to Intune will require 6 to 9 months of targeted operational friction. However, doing so eliminates our duplicate licensing costs, closes critical latency gaps in our Zero Trust security posture, and integrates our device fleet directly into Microsoft's AI ecosystem, future-proofing our architecture for the next decade."
